tel 1.0

# The words Soundness's documentation uses with a precise meaning. A term in brackets in prose —
# `[tactic]`, or `[tactics][tactic]` for an inflected form — shows its definition when pointed
# at, and leads to the page named by its `link`. doc/standards/style.md says when to add one.

define capability
  link  /doc/philosophy/capture-checking.md
  definition
      A value which must be in scope for an operation to be allowed, such as `Online` for a
      network request. A method that needs one says so in its signature, so what a method may
      do is visible in its type.

define choice package
  link  /doc/philosophy/declarative-context.md
  keyword policy import
  definition
      A package of alternative [givens][given] for one decision, such as `strategies`,
      `alphabets` or `httpBackends`. Importing one member by name makes that choice for
      everything in scope.

define compiletime
  definition
      While the program is being compiled. A check made at compiletime rejects wrong code
      before it can run; a compiletime error is reported by the compiler.

define contextual value
  link  /doc/philosophy/declarative-context.md
  keyword implicit
  definition
      A value the compiler finds in scope and passes to a method without it being written as
      an argument. One is declared with `given` and requested with `using`.

define derivation
  link  /doc/modules/derivation.md
  definition
      Having the compiler construct a [typeclass] instance for a case class or enumeration
      from the instances for its fields or cases, instead of writing it by hand.

define direct style
  link  /doc/philosophy/direct-style.md
  definition
      Code written as ordinary statements and expressions whose values are used directly,
      without wrapping results in a monad such as `Either`, `Try` or `Future`.

define enumeration
  definition
      A type declared with `enum` whose cases are a fixed list of values, known to the
      compiler, each with an ordinal giving its position in the declaration.

define given
  link  /doc/philosophy/declarative-context.md
  definition
      A definition marked `given`, which makes a [contextual value] available to any code
      that has it in scope.

define honest signature
  link  /doc/philosophy/honest-signatures.md
  definition
      A method signature that states everything the method needs and every way it can fail,
      so that nothing about calling it is left to documentation.

define immutable
  link  /doc/philosophy/immutability.md
  definition
      Never changing after construction. An operation that appears to alter an immutable
      value returns a new value and leaves the original as it was.

define impossible state
  link  /doc/philosophy/impossible-states.md
  definition
      A value that would be meaningless or invalid, and which the types are designed to make
      unrepresentable, so that no code needs to check for it.

define interpolator
  link  /doc/modules/interpolation.md
  definition
      A prefix on a string literal, such as `t"…"`, `url"…"` or `media"…"`, which checks the
      literal's content at [compiletime] and gives a value of a precise type.

define opaque type
  definition
      A type that is represented at runtime by another type, such as a `Long`, but which the
      compiler treats as distinct from it, so only the operations defined for it are allowed.

define optional
  link  /doc/modules/optional-values.md
  definition
      Typed `Optional[T]`: either a value of type `T`, or `Unset`. A present value is not
      wrapped, so it costs nothing more than the value itself.

define raises
  link  /doc/modules/errors.md#declaring-that-code-can-fail
  definition
      The infix type in a return type, as in `Text raises ParseError`, which declares that a
      method can fail with that error. It requires a [tactic] for the error from the caller.

define runtime
  definition
      While the program is running. A runtime error is one that the compiler could not rule
      out, and which is reported through a [tactic] when it happens.

define session
  definition
      A scope which opens a resource, such as a connection, lends it to the code inside the
      scope, and closes it when the scope ends.

define singleton type
  definition
      The type of exactly one value, such as the type of a particular [enumeration] case. A
      parameter of singleton type must be given a value the compiler can identify, not one
      computed at [runtime].

define strategy
  link  /doc/modules/errors.md#choosing-a-strategy
  keyword error handling
  definition
      A choice, in scope, of what happens when an operation fails: `throwUnsafely` throws an
      exception, `safely` gives `Unset`, `capture` returns the error. A strategy supplies the
      [tactic] a fallible method asks for.

define tactic
  link  /doc/modules/errors.md#declaring-that-code-can-fail
  definition
      A [contextual value] of type `Tactic[E]` which a method needs in order to report an
      error of type `E`. Whoever provides the tactic decides how the failure is handled.

define typeclass
  link  /doc/modules/derivation.md#products-sums-and-typeclasses
  definition
      A trait describing an ability a type may have, such as being shown, encoded or
      compared, with an instance for each type that has it. An instance is found as a
      [contextual value], so a type gains an ability without being modified. `Color is
      Showable` is the instance making `Color` showable.
